.SH DESCRIPTION
These routines match the patterns of
.IR sift (1)
against a document's tokens, for
.I sift
and
.IR carve (1).
.PP
.I Tokparse
makes a document's tokens from its UTF-8
.I text
and the answer a syntax program gives to the request
.B tokens
(see
.IR syn (4)):
a line per token of its start and length in runes, its names joined by commas, and the names of the
tags around it joined by commas, or
.BR - .
It sorts the tokens by their start.
It refuses, with nil and the error string set, a line that is not four or five fields, a start or
length that is not a decimal number, a token of no runes or past the text's end, tokens that
overlap, and partners that do not name each other or that cross.
Each
.B Tok
holds its runes
.B q0
to
.BR q1 ,
its text
.BR s ,
an
.B id
that two tokens share when their texts are equal,
its names and tag names, each a
.B Tnames
of
.B n
strings
.BR s ,
and in
.B link
the index of its partner, or \-1.
The partner is the token that starts at the rune a line's optional fifth field gives, if that
token's own fifth field names this one; a token with a partner is a bracket, whatever its text.
A partner that starts no token of the answer, as when the answer covers only part of the text,
leaves the token unpaired.
A token named
.B comment
or
.BI comment. x
has
.B comment
set.
.I Tokfree
frees them.
.I Tokat
gives the index of the first token that starts at rune
.I q
or after it;
.I tokbefore
gives the number of tokens that end at
.I q
or before it.
.I Tokhas
says whether one of the names
.I n
is
.I name
or begins with
.IB name . \f1.
.PP
.I Tokcomp
compiles a pattern, or returns nil and sets the error string.
A group and a bound name each capture, numbered from 0 in the order they open; a name is one
capture however often the pattern binds it.
.I Tokncap
gives the number of captures, at most
.BR Tmaxcap ,
and
.I tokcapname
the name of the
.IR i th,
or nil for a group.
.PP
.I Tokexec
finds the leftmost longest match among the tokens
.I t0
to
.IR t1 \-1
that starts at token
.I from
or after it.
A bracket whose partner lies outside them is never matched.
It fills
.IR m :
the match is the tokens
.B m->t0
to
.BR m->t1 \-1,
and
.BI m->cap[ i ]
holds the tokens the
.IR i th
capture matched, from
.BI m->cap[ i ][0]
to
.BI m->cap[ i ][1]\-1\f1,
or \-1 in both if it matched nothing.
A match that names no comment skips comments, so it neither starts nor ends on one.
.I Tokbexec
finds, among the same tokens, the match that ends last, and of those the longest:
the match that a search backward would find first.
Both return 1 if they find a match and 0 if not.
.PP
The caller provides
.I emalloc
and
.IR erealloc ,
which return zeroed memory and never nil.
A bad regular expression in a pattern calls
.I regerror
(see
.IR regexp (2));
a program that defines one that returns, and sets the error string, gets that string from
.IR tokcomp .
.PP
The compiler and the machine are those of
.IR sam (1)'s
regular expressions, on tokens.
An operator-precedence parser builds Thompson's fragments, numbering the captures as they open,
and compiles the pattern twice, the second time with its catenations reversed for
.IR tokbexec ,
which runs that program backward from
.IR t1 .
A thread is an instruction and its start, with its captures, its bound tokens and, for each
repetition around it, the partner of the outermost bracket it has entered.
The machine keeps one thread a state; a state is an instruction, those partners, and the texts of
the bound tokens, so two threads whose bound tokens have the same texts merge, and of two that
reach one state the one that started first stays, as in sam.
For a pattern of
.I m
instructions with
.I k
names, nested
.I s
repetitions deep, on
.I n
tokens with
.I V
distinct texts and brackets
.I D
deep, a search takes time at most proportional to
.I n
times
.I m
times
.IR V\u\s-2k\s0\d
times
.IR D\u\s-2s\s0\d .
A backward search with
.BI : x
runs the reversed program with looser guards, checks its match forward, and if that fails
searches forward from
.IR t0 .
